vil nor ugly.\n\nSo, P is true if either of these is true.\n\nLet me consider each part separately.\n\nFirst, Part 1: Harry is both evil and ugly.\n\nFrom premise 3, all evil people are ugly. So, if Harry is evil, he must be ugly. Therefore, if Harry is evil, he is automatically ugly. So, being both evil and ugly is consistent with the premises.\n\nNow, if Harry is evil, from premise 1, since he's evil, he's not kind. So, Harry is not kind.\n\nFrom premise 5, all CEOs are gentlemen. Harry is a gentleman (premise 6). But does that mean Harry is a CEO? Not necessarily. Because being a gentleman is a broader category than CEO. So, Harry could be a gentleman without being a CEO. Therefore, if Harry is evil, he's not kind, but we don't know if he's a CEO or not. Wait, but the statement Q says Harry is neither kind nor a CEO. So, if Harry is evil, he's not kind, but we don't know about the CEO part. So, in this case, Q would be \"Harry is neither kind nor a CEO.\" But we only know he's not kind; we don't know about the CEO status. So, if Harry is evil, he's not kind, but he could still be a CEO or not. Wait, but from premise 5, all CEOs are gentlemen, but Harry is a gentleman, so he could be a CEO or not. So, if Harry is evil, he's not kind, but he might still be a CEO. Therefore, in this case, Q would be false because Harry could be a CEO even if he's evil. Wait, but can Harry be both evil and a CEO?\n\nWait, let's think. If Harry is a CEO, from premise 5, he's a gentleman. From premise 4, he's handsome. From premise 2, no ugly person is handsome, so if Harry is a CEO, he's handsome, so he can't be ugly. But from premise 3, all evil people are ugly. So, if Harry is evil, he must be ugly. But if Harry is a CEO, he's handsome, so he can't be ugly. Therefore, Harry cannot be both evil and a CEO. Because if he's evil, he's ugly, but if he's a CEO, he's not ugly. So, Harry cannot be both evil and a CEO. Therefore, if Harry is evil, he's not a CEO. So, in this case, if Harry is evil, he's neither kind nor a CEO. So, Q would be true.\n\nWait, that's a bit confusing. Let me rephrase. If Harry is evil, he's ugly (from premise 3). But if Harry is a CEO, he's a gentleman (premise 5), which makes him handsome (premise 4). But from premise 2, no ugly person is handsome, so if Harry is a CEO, he can't be ugly. Therefore, if Harry is evil, he's ugly, which would mean he can't be a CEO because CEOs are not ugly. So, if Harry is evil, he's not a CEO. Therefore, if Harry is evil, he's neither kind nor a CEO. So, in this case, Q is true.\n\nNow, let's look at Part 2: Harry is neither evil nor ugly.\n\nIf Harry is neither evil nor ugly, then from premise 1, since he's not evil, he must be kind. So, Harry is kind.\n\nFrom premise 6, Harry is a gentleman, which from premise 4 makes him handsome. From premise 2, no ugly person is handsome, so Harry is not ugly. So, being neither evil nor ugly, Harry is kind and not ugly. Now, is Harry a CEO? Well, from premise 5, all CEOs are gentlemen, but not all gentlemen are CEOs. So, Harry could be a gentleman without being a CEO. Therefore, in this case, Harry is kind and may or may not be a CEO. So, the statement Q says Harry is neither kind nor a CEO. But in this scenario, Harry is kind, so Q would be false because he is kind. Therefore, if P is true (Harry is neither evil nor ugly), then Q is false because Harry is kind. So, the conditional statement \"If P, then Q\" would be false in this case because P is true, but Q is false.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. The statement is \"If Harry is either both evil and ugly, or neither evil nor ugly, then Harry is neither kind nor a CEO.\" So, if Harry is neither evil nor ugly, then according to the statement, he should be neither kind nor a CEO. But in reality, if Harry is neither evil nor ugly, he must be kind (from premise 1), so he is kind, making the conclusion of the statement false. Therefore, the entire conditional statement is false because there's a scenario where P is true, but Q is false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. If Harry is neither evil nor ugly, then from premise 1, he's kind. So, the conclusion that he's neither kind nor a CEO is false because he is kind. Therefore, the conditional statement is false because when P is true (Harry is neither evil nor ugly), Q is false.\n\nAlternatively, if Harry is both evil and ugly, then from premise 1, he's not kind, and from the earlier reasoning, he can't be a CEO because CEOs are not ugly. So, in that case, Q is true.\n\nBut since there's a scenario where P is true (Harry is neither evil nor ugly) and Q is false, the entire conditional statement is false. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I'm not missing anything. Let me outline all possibilities for Harry's status regarding evil and ugliness.\n\nHarry can be:\n\n1. Evil and ugly: From premise 3, all evil are ugly. So, if Harry is evil, he's ugly. From premise 1, he's not kind. From premise 6, he's a gentleman, so he's handsome (premise 4). But from premise 2, no ugly person is handsome, so if Harry is evil, he's ugly, which contradicts him being a gentleman (since gentlemen are handsome). Therefore, Harry cannot be evil because that would make him ugly, which would contradict him being a gentleman (handsome). So, Harry cannot be evil. Therefore, Harry must be kind.\n\nWait, that's a crucial point. Let me think again. Harry is a gentleman (premise 6), so he's handsome (premise 4). From premise 2, no ugly person is handsome, so Harry is not ugly. Therefore, Harry is not ugly. From premise 3, all evil people are ugly. Therefore, if Harry were evil, he would be ugly, but we know Harry is not ugly. Therefore, Harry cannot be evil. So, Harry must be kind.\n\nTherefore, Harry is kind and not evil. Also, since Harry is not evil, he's kind. Since Harry is a gentleman, he's not ugly (because if he were ugly, he couldn't be handsome, but he is a gentleman, so he's handsome, hence not ugly).\n\nSo, given that, let's revisit the original statement: \"If Harry is either both evil and ugly, or neither evil nor ugly, then Harry is neither kind nor a CEO.\"\n\nBut wait, we've just deduced that Harry cannot be evil because he's a gentleman (and thus not ugly), so the first part of P (Harry is both evil and ugly) is impossible. Therefore, P can only be true if Harry is neither evil nor ugly. But we've established that Harry is kind (not evil) and not ugly. So, P is true because Harry is neither evil nor ugly.\n\nNow, the conclusion Q is \"Harry is neither kind nor a CEO.\" But we know Harry is kind, so Q is false. Therefore, the conditional statement \"If P, then Q\" is false because P is true, but Q is false.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. Since Harry cannot be evil, the only way P is true is if Harry is neither evil nor ugly. But we've established that Harry is neither evil nor ugly, so P is true. Therefore, the statement is \"If P, then Q.\" Since P is true and Q is false, the entire statement is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement is false because Harry, being a gentleman, cannot be evil or ugly. Therefore, when P is true (Harry is neither evil nor ugly), Q is false (Harry is kind). \n\n**Answer: B) False**",
