      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement about Peter is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me list out all the given premises to make sure I understand them correctly.\n\n1. If people spill a lot of food on their clothing, then they do not tidy their spaces consistently and enjoy the process of cleaning. So, Spilling food \u2192 Not tidy and enjoy cleaning.\n\n2. All foodies who go out frequently to find new food restaurants to try are people who spill a lot of food on their clothing. So, Foodie (goes out frequently) \u2192 Spills food.\n\n3. If people grew up as only children with no siblings, then they tidy their spaces consistently and enjoy the process of cleaning. So, Only child \u2192 Tidy and enjoy cleaning.\n\n4. If people have parents who are very busy with their regular jobs, then they grew up as only children with no siblings. So, Busy parents \u2192 Only child.\n\n5. All households with a high net income have people who have parents who are very busy with their regular jobs. So, High net income \u2192 Busy parents.\n\n6. Peter is either both a person who spills a lot of food on his clothing and grew up as an only child with no siblings, or he is neither a person who spills a lot of food on his clothing nor grew up as an only child with no siblings. So, Peter is (Spills food \u2227 Only child) \u2228 (\u00acSpills food \u2227 \u00acOnly child).\n\nNow, the question is whether the statement \"Peter is either a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try or belongs to a household with a high net income\" is true, false, or uncertain.\n\nLet me try to map this out.\n\nFirst, let's consider what it means for Peter to be a foodie who goes out frequently. From premise 2, if someone is a foodie who goes out frequently, they spill a lot of food. So, Foodie \u2192 Spills food.\n\nNow, looking at premise 1, if someone spills food, then they do not tidy their spaces consistently and enjoy cleaning. So, Spills food \u2192 \u00acTidy \u2227 Enjoy cleaning.\n\nBut from premise 3, if someone grew up as an only child, they do tidy their spaces consistently and enjoy cleaning. So, Only child \u2192 Tidy \u2227 Enjoy cleaning.\n\nWait, that seems contradictory. Because if someone spills food, they don't tidy, but if they are an only child, they do tidy. So, can someone both spill food and be an only child? Let's see.\n\nFrom premise 6, Peter is either both a spiller and an only child, or neither. So, Peter is either (Spills \u2227 Only child) or (\u00acSpills \u2227 \u00acOnly child).\n\nBut wait, if Peter is both a spiller and an only child, then from premise 1, he doesn't tidy, but from premise 3, as an only child, he does tidy. That's a contradiction. So, can Peter be both?\n\nWait, that can't be. So, if Peter is an only child, he must tidy, but if he spills food, he doesn't tidy. Therefore, Peter cannot be both a spiller and an only child because that would create a contradiction.\n\nSo, premise 6 says Peter is either both or neither. But if being both leads to a contradiction, then Peter must be neither. Because if he were both, it would violate the premises. Therefore, Peter must be neither a spiller nor an only child.\n\nWait, but let me think again. Premise 6 says Peter is either both or neither. So, if being both leads to a contradiction, then the only possibility is that Peter is neither. Because the other option (both) is impossible.\n\nSo, Peter is neither a spiller nor an only child.\n\nNow, let's see what that implies.\n\nSince Peter is not a spiller, from premise 2, which says that foodies who go out frequently are spillers, so if Peter is not a spiller, he cannot be a foodie who goes out frequently. Because premise 2 is an implication: Foodie \u2192 Spiller. The contrapositive is \u00acSpiller \u2192 \u00acFoodie. So, if Peter doesn't spill, he's not a foodie.\n\nSo, Peter is not a foodie who goes out frequently.\n\nNow, what about the other part of the statement: does Peter belong to a household with a high net income?\n\nFrom premise 5, high net income \u2192 busy parents. From premise 4, busy parents \u2192 only child. So, high net income \u2192 only child.\n\nBut Peter is not an only child, as per our earlier conclusion. Therefore, Peter cannot belong to a household with a high net income because that would imply he's an only child, which he's not.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. Premise 5 says all households with high net income have people with busy parents. So, if Peter's household has a high net income, then Peter has busy parents. From premise 4, busy parents \u2192 only child. But Peter is not an only child, so Peter cannot have busy parents. Therefore, Peter's household cannot have a high net income because that would require busy parents, which would make him an only child, which he's not.\n\nSo, Peter is neither a foodie nor in a high-income household.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Peter is either a foodie who goes out frequently or belongs to a household with a high net income\" is false because he is neither.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Because sometimes in logic, the contrapositive can be tricky.\n\nPremise 2: Foodie \u2192 Spiller. So, \u00acSpiller \u2192 \u00acFoodie. Since Peter is \u00acSpiller, he's \u00acFoodie.\n\nPremise 5: High income \u2192 Busy parents. Premise 4: Busy parents \u2192 Only child. So, High income \u2192 Only child. But Peter is \u00acOnly child, so \u00acHigh income.\n\nTherefore, Peter is neither a foodie nor in a high-income household. So, the statement that he is either one or the other is false because he is neither.\n\nSo, the answer should be B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement is false because Peter is neither a foodie nor in a high-income household.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
