      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether Karen will share \"Stranger Things\" to Lisa based on the given context. Let me break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that \"Stranger Things\" is a popular Netflix show. Then, it mentions that if a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. So, since \"Stranger Things\" is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. That seems straightforward.\n\nNext, it says that if and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it. The phrase \"if and only if\" is a bit tricky, but I think it means that binge-watching is both a necessary and sufficient condition for downloading. So, Karen downloads a show exactly when she binge-watches it. That means if she binge-watches it, she will download it, and if she doesn't binge-watch it, she won't download it.\n\nThen, it's stated that Karen does not download \"Black Mirror.\" Since \"Black Mirror\" is a Netflix show, and Karen doesn't download it, that implies she didn't binge-watch it. Because according to the previous point, she only downloads shows she binge-watches. So, not downloading means she didn't binge-watch \"Black Mirror.\"\n\nNow, the question is about \"Stranger Things.\" Since it's popular, Karen will binge-watch it. And because she binge-watches it, she will download it. But the question isn't about downloading; it's about sharing it with Lisa. The context says that if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will share it to Lisa. So, since she binge-watched \"Stranger Things,\" she should share it with Lisa.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I'm not missing anything. The context doesn't mention anything about \"Stranger Things\" being downloaded or not, but since she binge-watched it, she must have downloaded it. But the sharing part is separate. The rule is that if she binge-watches, she shares it. So, regardless of downloading, the act of binge-watching triggers the sharing.\n\nSo, putting it all together: \"Stranger Things\" is popular \u2192 Karen binge-watches it \u2192 Karen downloads it (but that's not directly relevant here) \u2192 Karen shares it with Lisa. Therefore, the statement that Karen will share \"Stranger Things\" to Lisa is true.\n\nI don't see any contradictions or uncertainties here. All the conditions point towards Karen sharing it. So, the answer should be A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe statement is true because Karen will share \"Stranger Things\" with Lisa after binge-watching it.\n\n**Answer: A) True**",
