      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I've got this logical reasoning problem here, and I need to figure out if the statement \"Some Greeks are mortal\" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says two things: \"All humans are mortal\" and \"All Greeks are humans.\" I need to see how these two statements relate to each other and whether they support the conclusion that some Greeks are mortal.\n\nLet me start by understanding each statement. The first one, \"All humans are mortal,\" means that every single human being falls under the category of mortal beings. So, if something is a human, it must be mortal. That's straightforward.\n\nThe second statement is \"All Greeks are humans.\" This tells me that every Greek person is also a human. So, the set of Greeks is entirely contained within the set of humans. That means if I pick any Greek, they are automatically a human.\n\nNow, putting these together, since all Greeks are humans, and all humans are mortal, it follows that all Greeks must be mortal. Because if every Greek is a human, and every human is mortal, then every Greek is mortal. So, the statement \"All Greeks are mortal\" would be true.\n\nBut the question isn't asking if all Greeks are mortal; it's asking if some Greeks are mortal. Well, if all Greeks are mortal, then certainly some of them are. In fact, it's more than some; it's all of them. So, the statement \"Some Greeks are mortal\" must be true because it's a subset of the broader truth that all Greeks are mortal.\n\nWait, let me make sure I'm not missing anything. Could there be a scenario where the premises don't necessarily lead to the conclusion? For example, if the premises were about some humans being mortal and some Greeks being humans, then the conclusion might be uncertain. But in this case, the premises are universal: all humans are mortal, and all Greeks are humans. So, there's no room for doubt here. It's a straightforward syllogism.\n\nAnother way to look at it is using set theory. If I draw a Venn diagram, the set of humans would be entirely within the set of mortals. Then, the set of Greeks would be entirely within the set of humans. So, the Greeks set is entirely within the mortals set. Therefore, any part of the Greeks set is also within the mortals set, meaning some Greeks are definitely mortal.\n\nI think I'm confident now that the statement is true. There's no uncertainty because the premises are categorical and universal. So, the answer should be A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Some Greeks are mortal\" is true because all Greeks are humans, and all humans are mortal. Therefore, some (in fact, all) Greeks must be mortal.\n\n**Answer: A) True**",
