      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement \"Black Mirror is popular\" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context. Let me break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, the context mentions that \"Stranger Things\" is a popular Netflix show. Then, there are several conditional statements:\n\n1. If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it.\n2. If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it.\n3. Karen does not download \"Black Mirror.\"\n4. \"Black Mirror\" is a Netflix show.\n5. If Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will share it with Lisa.\n\nThe question is about the popularity of \"Black Mirror.\" So, I need to see if the given information allows us to determine whether it's popular or not.\n\nLet me start by listing the facts:\n\n- \"Stranger Things\" is popular.\n- Karen binge-watches popular Netflix shows.\n- Karen downloads a show if and only if she binge-watches it.\n- Karen does not download \"Black Mirror.\"\n- \"Black Mirror\" is on Netflix.\n- If Karen binge-watches a show, she shares it with Lisa.\n\nNow, let's analyze the implications.\n\nFrom point 3, Karen does not download \"Black Mirror.\" From point 2, she downloads a show only if she binge-watches it. So, if she didn't download it, that means she didn't binge-watch it. Because the \"if and only if\" condition implies that downloading is equivalent to binge-watching. So, no download means no binge-watch.\n\nIf Karen didn't binge-watch \"Black Mirror,\" then from point 1, which says that if a show is popular, she will binge-watch it. So, if she didn't binge-watch it, does that mean it's not popular? Because the statement is \"if a show is popular, then she binge-watches it.\" The contrapositive of this is \"if she doesn't binge-watch it, then it's not popular.\" So, yes, if she didn't binge-watch it, then it's not popular.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. The first statement is: If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. So, in logical terms, Popularity \u2192 Binge-watch. The contrapositive is \u00acBinge-watch \u2192 \u00acPopularity. So, if she didn't binge-watch it, then it's not popular.\n\nSince Karen didn't download \"Black Mirror,\" she didn't binge-watch it. Therefore, \"Black Mirror\" is not popular.\n\nWait, but hold on. The first statement is about popular shows leading to binge-watching. But does that mean that all shows she binge-watches are popular? Or could she binge-watch a show that's not popular? Because the first statement is only one direction. The second statement is an \"if and only if,\" meaning that she downloads a show exactly when she binge-watches it.\n\nSo, from the second statement: Download \u2194 Binge-watch. So, if she didn't download it, she didn't binge-watch it. From the first statement: Popularity \u2192 Binge-watch. So, if it were popular, she would have binge-watched it, which would mean she would have downloaded it. But she didn't download it, so it can't be popular.\n\nTherefore, \"Black Mirror\" is not popular. So the statement \"Black Mirror is popular\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me check again. The first statement is about popular shows leading to binge-watching, but it doesn't say that only popular shows are binge-watched. So, Karen could binge-watch a show that's not popular, but in that case, she would still download it because of the \"if and only if\" condition. But in this case, she didn't download \"Black Mirror,\" so she didn't binge-watch it. Therefore, since she didn't binge-watch it, and the first statement says that if it's popular, she would have, then it must not be popular.\n\nYes, that makes sense. So the answer should be B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Black Mirror is popular\" is false because Karen did not download it, implying she didn't binge-watch it, and thus it's not popular.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
