roach it differently.\n\nLet me consider the possibilities for Remy based on premise 6.\n\nPremise 6: Remy either (A) both goes to bed late and wakes up late and spends time tidying, or (B) neither goes to bed late nor wakes up late and doesn't spend time tidying.\n\nBut from premise 5: If Remy spends time tidying, then Remy does not go to bed late and does not wake up late. So, if Remy is in case A (tidying), then Remy cannot be going to bed late and waking up late, which contradicts case A. Therefore, case A is impossible because premise 5 negates it. So, Remy must be in case B: neither goes to bed late nor wakes up late, and doesn't spend time tidying.\n\nIf Remy doesn't spend time tidying, then from premise 3, which says that working at home implies tidying, the contrapositive would be that if Remy doesn't tidy, then Remy doesn't work at home. So, Remy doesn't work at home.\n\nIf Remy doesn't work at home, then from premise 1, which says owning a large monitor implies working at home, the contrapositive is that if Remy doesn't work at home, then Remy doesn't own a large monitor. So, Remy doesn't own a large monitor.\n\nFrom premise 2, having a fully stocked home office implies owning a large monitor. The contrapositive is that if Remy doesn't own a large monitor, then Remy doesn't have a fully stocked home office. So, Remy doesn't have a fully stocked home office.\n\nAdditionally, from premise 4, working at home implies visiting the office supply store. Since Remy doesn't work at home, Remy doesn't visit the store regularly.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Remy doesn't have a fully stocked home office and doesn't visit the office supply store regularly. Therefore, the statement that Remy has both is false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. If Remy doesn't work at home, then Remy doesn't have a fully stocked home office, and doesn't visit the store. So, the statement that Remy has both is false. So, the answer should be B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement is fal