ow, and false if he's not. But since we don't know, it's uncertain.\n\nWait, but in this case, Ben is funny, which doesn't necessarily mean he's from Simpsons. Unless being funny implies being from Simpsons? Let me check the statements again.\n\nFrom statement 3: Loved by children \u2192 Funny. So, if a character is loved by children, then it's funny. But the converse isn't necessarily true. So, being funny doesn't mean it's loved by children. Therefore, Ben being funny doesn't necessarily mean he's from Simpsons. So, he could be from Simpsons or not.\n\nIf he's from Simpsons, then from statement 4, he's loved by children, which makes him funny (which we already know). But if he's not from Simpsons, then he's just funny, but not necessarily loved by children. So, in this case, being funny doesn't give us any info about being yellow.\n\nSo, in this case, Ben is funny, so he's popular, not ugly. But we don't know if he's yellow. So, the statement \"ugly OR yellow\" would be true if he's yellow, false if he's not. So, again, uncertain.\n\nWait, but in the first case, when Ben is from Simpsons, he's funny, popular, not ugly, and possibly yellow. In the second case, Ben is funny, popular, not ugly, and possibly yellow. So, in both cases, Ben is not ugly, but we don't know if he's yellow. Therefore, the statement \"Ben is ugly or yellow\" reduces to \"Ben is yellow\" because he's not ugly. So, the question is whether Ben is yellow.\n\nBut from the given information, can we determine if Ben is yellow?\n\nIn the first case, Ben is from Simpsons. From statement 5, if he's yellow, then he's from Simpsons. But the converse isn't necessarily true. So, being from Simpsons doesn't mean he's yellow. So, he could be yellow or not.\n\nIn the second case, Ben is funny. There's no direct link between being funny and being yellow. So, again, he could be yellow or not.\n\nTherefore, in both cases, we don't have enough information to determine if Ben is yellow. So, the statement \"Ben is