not a basketball player). So, the implication is true \u2192 false, which is false.\n\nTherefore, the statement is false.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I'm not confusing anything. Let me go back to the premises.\n\nWe know that Yuri does not wear large clothes, so Yuri does not wear large shoes, so Yuri is not tall, so Yuri is not a basketball player.\n\nSo, Yuri is not a basketball player. Therefore, Yuri is not an American basketball player either because being an American basketball player is a subset of being a basketball player.\n\nSo, the statement is: If Yuri is not an American basketball player, then Yuri is a basketball player.\n\nBut since Yuri is not a basketball player, the conclusion is false. So, the implication is P \u2192 Q, where P is true (Yuri is not an American basketball player) and Q is false (Yuri is not a basketball player). Therefore, the implication is false.\n\nSo, the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me think again. Because sometimes in logic, the way we structure the statements can be tricky. Let me try to represent this with a truth table.\n\nP: Yuri is not an American basketball player.\n\nQ: Yuri is a basketball player.\n\nWe know that Yuri is not a basketball player, so Q is false.\n\nWe also know that Yuri is not a basketball player, so P is true because being not an American basketball player is a broader category that includes not being a basketball player at all.\n\nSo, in the implication P \u2192 Q, P is true, Q is false. Therefore, the implication is false.\n\nYes, that makes sense. So, the statement is false.\n</think>\n\nThe statement is false because Yuri is not a basketball player, making the implication's consequent false while the antecedent is true.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
