rs, and all professional tennis players are athletes. So, Grand Slam champions are a subset of athletes. But Djokovic is an athlete, but we don't know if he's a professional tennis player. He could be a different kind of athlete, like a football player or something else.\n\nBut wait, the context also says that if a person is a Grand Slam champion, they are a professional tennis player. So, if Djokovic were a Grand Slam champion, he would be a professional tennis player. But the context doesn't say that all professional tennis players are Grand Slam champions, only that all Grand Slam champions are professional tennis players.\n\nSo, Djokovic is an athlete, which could mean he's a professional tennis player (and thus a Grand Slam champion) or another type of athlete. But the context doesn't provide any information about whether Djokovic is a professional tennis player or not. It only tells us that he's famous and an athlete.\n\nTherefore, based on the given information, we can't definitively say whether Djokovic is a Grand Slam champion or not. He could be, but he might also be another type of athlete. So, the statement is uncertain.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"Djokovic is a Grand Slam champion\" is uncertain because while he is an athlete, the context doesn't specify whether he is a professional tennis player, which is required to be a Grand Slam champion.\n\n# Answer:\nC) Uncertain",
